        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "d7e9e5da-e1ed-5e80-8772-339505551530",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Ships: HMS Warspite, Queen Elizabeth-class battleships, Iron Duke-class, König-class, Bayern-class\n- Organizations: Royal Navy\n- Concepts: battleship classes, super-dreadnoughts, naval capabilities, world wars, ocean theaters\n\nFacts:\n- The Royal Navy commissioned the five Queen Elizabeth-class battleships in 1915–1916 as super-dreadnoughts.\n- The Queen Elizabeth-class battleships had superior firepower, protection, and speed compared with the Royal Navy's earlier Iron Duke-class battleships and earlier German classes such as the König class.\n- The German Bayern-class ships were competitive with the Queen Elizabeth class, but they were 2 knots slower.\n- HMS Warspite served for thirty years.\n- HMS Warspite's career spanned both World War I and World War II.\n- HMS Warspite operated across the Atlantic, Indian, Arctic, and Pacific Oceans.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "025379fe-937c-5772-bb16-aa0a07c28dc7",
        "text": "Page: HMS Warspite (03). Sentence: Her thirty-year career covered both world wars and took her across the Atlantic, Indian, Arctic and Pacific Oceans.\nPage: Queen Elizabeth-class battleship. Sentence: The \"Queen Elizabeth\"-class battleships were a class of five super-dreadnoughts of the Royal Navy commissioned in 1915–16.\nPage: Queen Elizabeth-class battleship. Sentence: These battleships were superior in firepower, protection and speed to their Royal Navy predecessors of the \"Iron Duke\" class as well as preceding German classes such as the \"König\" class , although the corresponding \"Bayern\"-class ships were competitive except for being 2 kn slower.",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
      },
